To make the circle element look more 3D, you can apply some CSS 3D transforms and shadows. Here's an updated version of the `.circle` styles:

```css
.circle {
  fill: radial-gradient(white, lightblue);
  filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px gray);
  transform-style: preserve-3d;
  transform: translateZ(10px) rotateX(-10deg) rotateY(5deg);
  transition: transform 0.3s ease-in-out;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc);
  box-shadow: 10px 10px 5px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75);
  border-radius: 70%;
}
```

Here's how these styles affect the circle:

1. `transform-style: preserve-3d;` tells browsers that we want to treat this element as if it's in 3D space, with multiple surfaces that can be transformed independently.

2. `transform:` applies some transforms to the element:

  - `translateZ(10px)` moves the element forward by 10 pixels along the z-axis (which is perpendicular to the screen). This gives it some depth.
  - `rotateX(-10deg)` rotates the element around its x-axis (-10 degrees in this case), which adds some perspective.
  - `rotateY(5deg)` does the same for the y-axis (5 degrees in this case).

3. `transition: transform 0.3s ease-in-out;` makes the transformation smooth and gradual over a duration of 0.3 seconds.

With these changes, the circle will appear more 3D and have a sense of depth and perspective. You can adjust the values of the `translateZ()`, `rotateX()`, and `rotateY()` functions to achieve different effects. Experiment with them until you find what looks best!

