This implementation uses the `setdiff` function to find the set difference between the two sets of words, which efficiently returns the elements that are unique to the first set (`words1`) and not present in the second set (`words2`). This operation is performed on the lowercased and split-into-words versions of both texts (`text1` and `text2`) to ensure case insensitivity and proper handling of multiple spaces between words. The resulting unique words are stored in the `uniqueWords` variable, which can be printed or further processed as needed. To improve performance for very large text files, consider using memory-mapped files instead of loading the entire contents into memory at once. This can significantly reduce the amount of memory required and improve overall speed. Here's how you can modify the code to use memory-mapped files:
