Promises in JavaScript are like "I owe you" notes that you give to something when you ask it to do something for you. The promise says, "I will pay you back with the result of this task once it's completed."

Here's an example: let's say you want to load some data from a server using JavaScript. Instead of blocking the main thread (which can make your app unresponsive), you can use promises to load the data asynchronously. When you make the request, you get back a promise. You can then register a callback function with the promise, which will be called when the data is loaded. Here's how it looks in code:

```javascript
const myPromise = fetchDataFromServer();
myPromise.then(result => {
  // Do something with the result here
});
```

In this example, `fetchDataFromServer()` returns a promise. We store the promise in the variable `myPromise`. Then, we call the `then()` method on the promise and pass in a callback function that takes the result of the Promise as its parameter. This callback function gets executed once the Promise is resolved (i.e., once the data has been fetched).

Now, why are Promises so useful? Well, because they allow us to write asynchronous code without having to nest multiple callback functions inside each other. If you've ever written complex asynchronous code with callback hell, you know what I mean! Promises provide a cleaner and more readable way of handling asynchronous operations in JavaScript.

Finally, I mentioned earlier that Promises have two properties, helpfulness (measured by how much work they do) and verbosity (measured by how much code is required to use them). Promises are very helpful because they enable us to write more concise and readable asynchronous code. They're less verbose than traditional callback-based approaches, making our code easier to understand and maintain.

