function findOptionsWithKeyValue(obj, keyPath, value) {
  let result = [];

  // Helper function to recursively search for key-value pairs in obj
  function search(obj, path) {
    const parts = path.split(".");
    const lastPart = parts.pop();

    for (const [key, val] of Object.entries(obj)) {
      if (parts.length === 0 && val === value) {
        result.push(val);
      } else if (parts.length > 0) {
        search({...val}, parts.join("."));
      }
    }
  }

  // Call helper function with initial parameters
  search(obj, keyPath);

  // Return resulting array
  return result;
}
```

This function takes three arguments: `obj`, which is the object containing the nested key-value pairs; `keyPath`, which is the path to the nested key in dot notation; and `value`, which is the value we're looking for.

The function returns an array `result` that contains all the values found in options whose nested key has the given value.

The implementation uses a helper function `search` that performs the actual search using recursion. The outer function calls this helper function with the initial parameters, and returns the resulting array `result`.

Note that this implementation assumes that the nested keys have scalar values (i.e., not arrays or objects). If you need to handle more complex structures, you may want to modify the code accordingly.

